window.PEDS_DATA = {"note":"Which of this notebook's conditions present in childhood, in which age band, and what changes about them in a child — a tagging of existing entries. The reference sections were written for this tool from standard paediatric references; they are not from the coursework and are not peer reviewed.","ages":[{"id":"neonate","label":"Neonate (0–28 days)"},{"id":"infant","label":"Infant (1–12 months)"},{"id":"toddler","label":"Toddler (1–3 years)"},{"id":"child","label":"Child (3–12 years)"},{"id":"adolescent","label":"Adolescent (12–18 years)"},{"id":"any","label":"Any age in childhood"}],"conditions":[{"condition":"Kawasaki disease","age":"child","angle":"Fever of five days or more with four of five criteria. The leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in the US and Japan — echocardiography and IVIG within 10 days, not a naturopathic problem."},{"condition":"Scarlet fever","age":"child","angle":"Sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue and Pastia's lines after a streptococcal pharyngitis. Antibiotics prevent rheumatic fever; this is a prescription, not a preference."},{"condition":"Rheumatic fever","age":"child","angle":"Jones criteria two to four weeks after untreated group A strep. Carditis is the finding that decides the child's future."},{"condition":"Measles","age":"child","angle":"Koplik spots precede the rash by a day or two. Notifiable, and vitamin A reduces mortality in deficiency; airborne precautions for 4 days after rash onset."},{"condition":"Rubella","age":"child","angle":"Mild in the child, catastrophic in the first-trimester fetus — the exposure history of every pregnant contact matters more than the child's own course."},{"condition":"Varicella","age":"child","angle":"Crops of lesions at different stages. Never aspirin — Reye's syndrome. Watch for secondary bacterial infection of excoriated lesions."},{"condition":"Roseola","age":"infant","angle":"Three to five days of high fever in a well-looking infant, then the rash appears as the fever breaks. The commonest cause of a first febrile seizure."},{"condition":"Fifth disease","age":"child","angle":"Slapped-cheek then lacy reticular rash; no longer infectious once the rash appears. Aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease and hydrops in pregnancy are the real risks."},{"condition":"Hand, foot and mouth disease","age":"toddler","angle":"Coxsackie A16 or enterovirus 71. Oral ulcers drive refusal to drink — the management question is hydration, not the rash."},{"condition":"Non-specific viral exanthem","age":"child","angle":"The commonest paediatric rash by far, and a diagnosis of exclusion once the specific exanthems and meningococcal disease are ruled out."},{"condition":"Otitis media","age":"toddler","angle":"Peak incidence 6 to 24 months. A bulging, immobile tympanic membrane, not just a red one. Watchful waiting with analgesia is appropriate in the older, well child."},{"condition":"Impetigo","age":"child","angle":"Honey-crusted lesions, highly contagious in nurseries. Topical treatment for limited disease; keep the child off until 48 hours of treatment or crusting."},{"condition":"Sore throat","age":"child","angle":"Centor is unreliable under 3. Test before treating, and remember that a strawberry tongue changes the diagnosis."},{"condition":"Common cold","age":"child","angle":"Six to eight a year is normal in a nursery-going child. No cough or cold preparation is licensed under 6, and honey is contraindicated under 1 (infant botulism)."},{"condition":"Influenza","age":"child","angle":"Children shed longer and higher. Never aspirin. Watch for secondary bacterial pneumonia at the second fever."},{"condition":"Pneumonia","age":"child","angle":"Tachypnoea is the most sensitive sign — count the respiratory rate for a full minute against the age-band table below. Chest radiography is not needed in uncomplicated community-acquired disease."},{"condition":"Bronchitis","age":"infant","angle":"Under 2, the picture is usually bronchiolitis rather than bronchitis: RSV, widespread crackles and wheeze, and feeding difficulty as the admission criterion."},{"condition":"Urinary tract infection","age":"infant","angle":"In the pre-verbal child, a UTI presents as fever without a source, vomiting or poor feeding. A clean catch or catheter specimen — bag urine is not diagnostic — and imaging after the first proven infection."},{"condition":"Sexually transmitted infection","age":"adolescent","angle":"In a prepubertal child, a sexually transmitted infection is a safeguarding matter before it is a microbiological one."},{"condition":"Diaper dermatitis","age":"infant","angle":"Irritant dermatitis spares the skin folds; candidal infection involves them and has satellite lesions. That distinction decides the treatment."},{"condition":"Eczema","age":"infant","angle":"Face and extensor surfaces in infancy, flexural from toddlerhood. Emollients are the treatment; restricting a child's diet on an IgG panel risks nutritional harm for little gain."},{"condition":"Seborrheic dermatitis","age":"infant","angle":"Cradle cap: greasy yellow scale on the scalp of a well infant, self-limiting over weeks. Loosen with oil and a soft brush; ketoconazole shampoo only if extensive."},{"condition":"Molluscum contagiosum","age":"child","angle":"Self-limiting over months to two years. Most children need reassurance and time, not a procedure."},{"condition":"Warts","age":"child","angle":"Two-thirds resolve within two years untreated. Salicylate and occlusion first."},{"condition":"Head lice","age":"child","angle":"Diagnosis needs a living louse, not nits alone. Wet combing and a topical agent; treat the household, not the classroom."},{"condition":"Scabies","age":"child","angle":"Infants get palm, sole and scalp involvement that older children do not. Treat every household contact on the same day, and warn that the itch outlasts the mite by weeks."},{"condition":"Tinea","age":"child","angle":"Tinea capitis needs oral treatment — topicals do not reach the follicle — and a look at the family and any pets."},{"condition":"Keratosis pilaris","age":"child","angle":"Follicular roughness on the outer arms and cheeks. Benign, familial, and largely cosmetic; emollients and patience."},{"condition":"Pityriasis alba","age":"child","angle":"Hypopigmented dry patches on the face of an atopic child, more visible after sun exposure. Not vitiligo and not tinea."},{"condition":"Pityriasis rosea","age":"adolescent","angle":"Herald patch then a Christmas-tree distribution. Self-limiting over 6 to 8 weeks; exclude secondary syphilis in the sexually active adolescent."},{"condition":"Acne","age":"adolescent","angle":"Treat early and treat scarring aggressively. Screen mood before and during isotretinoin, and never use tetracyclines under 8 years or in pregnancy."},{"condition":"Urticaria and angioedema","age":"child","angle":"Acute urticaria in a child is usually post-viral, not allergic. Facial or tongue swelling with breathing difficulty is anaphylaxis until proven otherwise."},{"condition":"Henoch-Schonlein purpura","age":"child","angle":"Palpable purpura on the buttocks and legs, arthritis, abdominal pain. Check urine for blood and protein weekly for six months — the kidney is what determines outcome."},{"condition":"Incontinentia pigmenti","age":"infant","angle":"X-linked dominant; the four skin stages evolve over infancy. Ophthalmology and neurology review, because the eye and brain are involved more often than the skin suggests."},{"condition":"Erythema multiforme","age":"child","angle":"Target lesions, usually post-herpetic. Mucosal involvement moves it toward Stevens-Johnson and out of outpatient care."},{"condition":"Erythema nodosum","age":"adolescent","angle":"Tender shin nodules; in a child, look for streptococcus, tuberculosis and inflammatory bowel disease."},{"condition":"Burns","age":"child","angle":"Use the age-adjusted body surface area chart — the paediatric head is proportionally far larger. Any burn with an inconsistent history is a safeguarding referral."},{"condition":"Wound healing","age":"child","angle":"Children heal fast and scar readily; sun protection of a new scar for a year."},{"condition":"Asthma","age":"child","angle":"Diagnosis under 5 is clinical — spirometry is unreliable before school age. Inhaler technique with a spacer outranks every dose adjustment, and check growth on inhaled steroids."},{"condition":"Allergic rhinitis","age":"child","angle":"Allergic shiners, the transverse nasal crease and mouth breathing. Untreated, it worsens asthma control and disturbs sleep."},{"condition":"Cystic fibrosis","age":"infant","angle":"Newborn screening then sweat chloride. Failure to thrive with steatorrhoea and recurrent chest infection is the classic missed presentation."},{"condition":"Cough","age":"child","angle":"A cough over 4 weeks in a child needs a cause, not a syrup. Consider inhaled foreign body, pertussis, asthma and bronchiectasis."},{"condition":"Obstructive sleep apnea","age":"child","angle":"Adenotonsillar hypertrophy, not obesity, is the usual cause. Presents as behavioural difficulty, bedwetting and poor growth rather than daytime sleepiness."},{"condition":"Sinusitis","age":"child","angle":"The frontal sinuses do not pneumatise until 7 to 8 years — frontal tenderness in a young child is something else."},{"condition":"GERD","age":"infant","angle":"Physiological posseting in a thriving infant needs reassurance, not a proton pump inhibitor. Back-arching, refusal to feed, faltering growth or blood change that."},{"condition":"Constipation","age":"child","angle":"Functional in the great majority. Disimpact first, then maintain for months — stopping early is why it recurs. Delayed meconium, ribbon stools or failure to thrive point elsewhere."},{"condition":"Diarrhea","age":"toddler","angle":"Toddler's diarrhoea in a thriving child needs fat in the diet and less fruit juice. In the unwell child, dehydration assessment governs everything."},{"condition":"Celiac disease","age":"child","angle":"Faltering growth, abdominal distension and irritability after weaning onto gluten. Test on a gluten-containing diet, and check the total IgA."},{"condition":"Food intolerance","age":"child","angle":"Distinguish IgE allergy — minutes, reproducible, anaphylaxis risk — from delayed intolerance. Elimination diets in children carry a real nutritional cost."},{"condition":"Inflammatory bowel disease","age":"adolescent","angle":"Growth failure and delayed puberty may precede any bowel symptom by years. Plot the growth chart."},{"condition":"Parasites, intestinal","age":"child","angle":"Pinworm presents as nocturnal perianal itch and disturbed sleep; treat the household and repeat at two weeks."},{"condition":"Functional abdominal pain (pediatric)","age":"child","angle":"Periumbilical, no red flags, normal growth and examination. The diagnosis is positive, not one of exclusion by investigation."},{"condition":"Cyclical vomiting syndrome","age":"child","angle":"Stereotyped attacks with well intervals, often with a migraine family history."},{"condition":"Nausea and vomiting","age":"infant","angle":"Bilious vomiting in an infant is malrotation with volvulus until surgery says otherwise. Projectile vomiting at 3 to 6 weeks is pyloric stenosis."},{"condition":"Gastroschisis","age":"neonate","angle":"Antenatally diagnosed, surgically managed. The postoperative questions are gut function and nutrition."},{"condition":"Appendicitis","age":"child","angle":"Presents atypically under 5 and perforates earlier. Serial examination beats a single score."},{"condition":"Pediatric hypertension","age":"child","angle":"Normal is a percentile, not a number — read the child's blood pressure against age, sex and height. Under 6, hypertension is secondary until proven otherwise, usually renal."},{"condition":"Obesity","age":"child","angle":"Plot BMI centile, not BMI. Weight-neutral language, family-level change, and screening for insulin resistance, fatty liver and sleep apnoea."},{"condition":"Diabetes mellitus, type 1","age":"child","angle":"Polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss in a child is type 1 diabetes until a glucose says otherwise — same-day capillary glucose and ketones, never a routine appointment."},{"condition":"Anemia, iron deficiency","age":"toddler","angle":"Excess cow's milk is the commonest cause after the first year. Iron deficiency without anaemia already affects development."},{"condition":"Kidney stones","age":"adolescent","angle":"Uncommon enough in childhood to warrant metabolic screening after the first stone."},{"condition":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome","age":"child","angle":"Bloody diarrhoea followed by pallor, oliguria and bruising. Avoid antibiotics and antimotility agents in E. coli O157 colitis — they raise the risk."},{"condition":"Tetralogy of Fallot","age":"infant","angle":"Tet spells: knee-to-chest position, oxygen, morphine and fluids. A murmur that softens during a spell is a worsening sign."},{"condition":"Ventricular septal defect","age":"infant","angle":"The loudest murmurs are the smallest defects. Faltering growth and sweating with feeds signal a significant shunt."},{"condition":"Atrial septal defect","age":"child","angle":"Fixed split second heart sound, often silent until adulthood."},{"condition":"Coarctation of the aorta","age":"neonate","angle":"Radiofemoral delay and a blood pressure difference between arm and leg. Collapse when the duct closes."},{"condition":"Transposition of the great arteries","age":"neonate","angle":"Cyanosis in the first day of life that does not correct with oxygen. Prostaglandin keeps the duct open until surgery."},{"condition":"Neonatal abstinence syndrome","age":"neonate","angle":"Scored, not guessed — Finnegan or ESC. Non-pharmacological care first: rooming-in, feeding on cue, swaddling and low stimulation."},{"condition":"Lactation support","age":"infant","angle":"Assess the latch before anything else. Weight, output and maternal pain are the working measures."},{"condition":"Seizure disorder","age":"child","angle":"A simple febrile seizure between 6 months and 6 years needs no imaging or EEG. Focal, prolonged or recurrent features within one illness change that."},{"condition":"Attention deficit disorder","age":"child","angle":"Symptoms in two or more settings before 12, with functional impairment. Screen sleep, hearing and vision before attributing anything to attention."},{"condition":"Anxiety","age":"child","angle":"Presents as somatic complaint, school refusal and irritability rather than as stated worry."},{"condition":"Depression","age":"adolescent","angle":"Irritability may replace low mood. Ask directly about self-harm, and never take the parent's account as the whole picture — see the adolescent alone."},{"condition":"Insomnia","age":"child","angle":"Behavioural sleep problems, not primary insomnia. Melatonin is for circadian shift, not for a bedtime battle."},{"condition":"Bipolar disorder","age":"adolescent","angle":"Screen before starting an antidepressant: an antidepressant alone can precipitate mania."}],"sections":[{"id":"vitals","title":"Normal vital signs by age","body":["Neonate (0–28 days): heart rate 100–180, respiratory rate 30–60, systolic BP 60–90 mmHg.","Infant (1–12 months): heart rate 100–160, respiratory rate 30–53, systolic BP 72–104 mmHg.","Toddler (1–3 years): heart rate 90–150, respiratory rate 22–37, systolic BP 86–106 mmHg.","Preschool (3–5 years): heart rate 80–140, respiratory rate 20–28, systolic BP 89–112 mmHg.","School age (6–11 years): heart rate 70–120, respiratory rate 18–25, systolic BP 97–120 mmHg.","Adolescent (12–18 years): heart rate 60–100, respiratory rate 12–20, systolic BP 110–131 mmHg.","Rough rules: expected systolic BP = 90 + (2 × age in years); the 5th centile, below which is hypotension, = 70 + (2 × age in years). Hypotension is a late and pre-terminal sign in a child — tachycardia, prolonged capillary refill and reduced consciousness come first.","Count the respiratory rate over a full minute in a calm child. Tachypnoea is the most sensitive sign of pneumonia and of shock."],"flag":null},{"id":"dosing","title":"Age- and weight-based dosing rules","body":["Weight-based dosing (mg/kg) is the standard and should be used wherever a paediatric dose exists. The rules below are historical approximations for when it does not, and none of them is a prescribing authority.","Clark's rule: child dose = adult dose × (weight in pounds ÷ 150). The most defensible of the three, because it uses weight.","Young's rule: child dose = adult dose × age in years ÷ (age + 12). For children 2 to 12 years.","Fried's rule: infant dose = adult dose × age in months ÷ 150. For infants under 2 years.","Body surface area: child dose = adult dose × (BSA in m² ÷ 1.73). The most accurate for cytotoxics and narrow-therapeutic-index drugs. Mosteller: BSA = √(height in cm × weight in kg ÷ 3600).","Herbal tinctures follow the same logic. Clark's rule applied to an adult dose of 30 drops gives a 20 kg (44 lb) child roughly 9 drops. Give alcohol-based tinctures in hot water to flash off the alcohol, or use a glycerite for children under 12.","Never scale an adult dose of a low-dose botanical this way. Those maxima are toxicity thresholds, not efficacy targets — see the Low-Dose Reference tab."],"flag":"Written for this tool. Weight-based paediatric dosing from a current formulary always outranks any of these rules."},{"id":"redflags","title":"Red flags by age","body":["Any fever in an infant under 28 days is a full septic screen and admission, however well the baby looks. Between 1 and 3 months, the threshold for investigation stays very low.","A non-blanching rash with fever is meningococcal disease until proven otherwise — this is an ambulance, not an appointment.","Bilious (green) vomiting in an infant is malrotation with volvulus until surgery says otherwise.","Bulging fontanelle, high-pitched cry, or a fontanelle that is sunken with reduced skin turgor.","Grunting, head bobbing, nasal flaring, tracheal tug, intercostal or subcostal recession — respiratory distress that is compensating, and will not compensate for long.","A child who is not interested in their surroundings, will not smile, or cannot be roused to a normal state. Parental report that 'this is not my child' outranks any score.","Reduced urine output — fewer than four wet nappies a day in an infant, or no urine for 8 hours in an older child.","Faltering growth crossing two centile lines, or any weight loss in an infant.","A limp or a refusal to weight-bear, with or without fever: septic arthritis and osteomyelitis first.","An injury whose mechanism does not fit the history, the developmental stage, or the pattern of bruising — a safeguarding referral, made the same day."],"flag":null},{"id":"dehydration","title":"Assessing dehydration","body":["No clinically detectable dehydration (under 5%): alert, normal pulse and skin turgor, moist mucous membranes, normal urine output. Oral fluids and continued feeding.","Clinical dehydration (5–10%): irritable or lethargic, mildly tachycardic, sunken eyes, dry mucous membranes, reduced urine output, capillary refill under 2 seconds still. Oral rehydration solution, 50 mL/kg over 4 hours plus maintenance, in small frequent volumes.","Shock (over 10%): decreased consciousness, pale or mottled, cold extremities, tachycardia, weak pulses, capillary refill over 2 seconds, hypotension as a late sign. Intravenous bolus 10–20 mL/kg, and escalate.","Maintenance fluid, the 4-2-1 rule: 4 mL/kg/hour for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/hour for the next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/hour for every kilogram beyond 20.","Weight change is the most accurate measure of fluid deficit there is — a recent well-child weight is worth more than any examination sign."],"flag":null},{"id":"milestones","title":"Developmental milestones and the red flags that go with them","body":["6 weeks: fixes and follows, smiles responsively, startles to noise. Red flag — no social smile by 10 weeks.","6 months: sits with support, reaches and transfers, babbles, turns to a voice. Red flag — persistent primitive reflexes, hand preference before 12 months (suggests hemiplegia).","9–12 months: sits unsupported, pincer grip, waves and points, says one or two words with meaning. Red flag — not sitting by 12 months.","18 months: walks steadily, 6 to 20 words, scribbles, feeds with a spoon. Red flag — not walking by 18 months.","2 years: runs, two-word phrases, half of speech intelligible to a stranger, symbolic play. Red flag — no words by 18 months, no phrases by 2 years, any loss of skills at any age.","3 years: climbs stairs alternating feet, three-word sentences, copies a circle, plays with other children.","4–5 years: hops, copies a cross then a square, counts, dresses independently.","Regression — the loss of a skill once acquired — is a red flag at every age and always warrants specialist referral."],"flag":null},{"id":"fever","title":"Fever in a child","body":["The height of a fever does not grade the seriousness of the illness after the neonatal period. How the child looks between fevers does.","Treat for distress, not for the number. Paracetamol 15 mg/kg 4- to 6-hourly (maximum 4 doses in 24 hours) or ibuprofen 5–10 mg/kg 6- to 8-hourly with food. Do not alternate routinely, and do not use ibuprofen in dehydration or varicella.","Never aspirin under 16 years outside of Kawasaki disease and specialist rheumatology — Reye's syndrome.","Fever without a source in a child under 3 needs a urine sample. It is the commonest occult bacterial infection at that age.","A febrile seizure between 6 months and 6 years, lasting under 15 minutes, generalised, and with a full recovery within an hour, needs no imaging or EEG — and does not mean epilepsy."],"flag":null},{"id":"prescribing","title":"Botanicals and supplements in children","body":["No honey under 1 year — infant botulism.","No aspirin or salicylate-containing herbs in a febrile child under 16 — Reye's syndrome. That includes willow bark and meadowsweet.","Essential oils are not for internal use in children, and peppermint or eucalyptus applied near the face of an infant can cause laryngospasm.","Avoid alcohol-extracted tinctures where possible under 12: use a glycerite, or add the tincture to hot water and let it stand to drive off the alcohol.","Vitamin D: 400 IU daily from birth for breastfed infants, per most national guidance. Preformed vitamin A is easy to over-give in drops, and is teratogenic in the adolescent who may be pregnant.","Iron is the leading cause of fatal poisoning in small children. Dispense with that warning, every time.","Choking risk: no whole nuts, no hard sweets and no capsules that must be swallowed whole under about 4 years.","Weigh the child at every visit and dose from that weight — not from the last recorded one, and not from age."],"flag":null}]};
